---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/static/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { href: '/', label: 'Home' },
  { href: '/about/', label: 'About' },
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Open Mermaid"
  description="Open Mermaid is a standalone, local-first browser workspace for creating Mermaid diagrams without an account or backend."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <div class="shell">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />
    <header class="page-hero page-hero--compact measure">
      <p class="eyebrow">Independent and standalone</p>
      <h1>About Open Mermaid</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A focused Mermaid workspace that stays useful even when every optional platform connection
        is absent.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-layout">
      <article class="article-content">
        <section>
          <h2>Why it exists</h2>
          <p>
            Text-based diagrams are portable, reviewable, and easy to keep with the work they
            explain. Open Mermaid gives that source a modern browser workspace without turning basic
            editing into a hosted service. It is designed for beginners who want guided starts and
            developers who want direct control of valid Mermaid syntax.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>What standalone means</h2>
          <p>
            Version 1 is a static application. It needs a network connection to load its site
            assets, then processes diagrams and keeps its document library locally in the browser.
            There is no account system, document backend, AI API, ChatGPT dependency, service
            worker, or runtime content-delivery network. Some deployments may offer optional
            aggregate template-use reporting; it is off until you opt in and never includes diagram
            content.
          </p>
          <p>
            A future ChatGPT App, AI generator, cloud sync service, or collaboration service may
            consume the same validated document contract. Those capabilities must remain separately
            deployed and optional; the core web application cannot depend on them.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Available without a paid key</h2>
          <p>
            The standalone editor requires no account or paid API key. Its core editing, rendering,
            local-library, sharing, and export workflows run directly in the browser without a
            separately hosted application service.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>A measured roadmap</h2>
          <p>
            Version 1 focuses on local authoring, sharing, and export. Accounts, cloud sync,
            collaboration, AI, PDF and JPEG export, and offline support can be evaluated separately
            without weakening the privacy promise, performance budget, or security boundary. Future
            work must earn its place with a clear user benefit and explicit data-flow disclosure.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Name and affiliation</h2>
          <p>
            Open Mermaid is an independent project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or
            sponsored by the Mermaid project or OpenAI. Project maintainers must complete name,
            domain, and trademark clearance before a public launch; this website does not claim that
            clearance has occurred.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
      <aside class="article-aside">
        <h2>Project principles</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Standalone first</li>
          <li>Local processing by default</li>
          <li>Accessible authoring guidance</li>
          <li>Strict security boundaries</li>
          <li>Portable static deployment</li>
          <li>Truthful discoverability</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href="/editor/">Open the editor</a></p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
